1
00:00:19,048 --> 00:00:20,411
Good evening, everybody.

2
00:00:20,431 --> 00:00:22,213
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:22,594 --> 00:00:25,559
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:41,968
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa

5
00:00:42,656 --> 00:00:58,472
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambuddhasa homage to the Buddha the blessed noble and fully self-awakened one.

6
00:00:58,512 --> 00:01:08,022
So I thought this evening we'd have a look at number 66 which is the simile of the quail which is only one of the similes that the Buddha uses.

7
00:01:08,522 --> 00:01:11,144
So this discourse is about renunciation

8
00:01:11,698 --> 00:01:12,799
and about the fetters.

9
00:01:12,941 --> 00:01:21,070
So what we don't renounce becomes a fetter, a chain, something that keeps us locked into this existence.

10
00:01:22,292 --> 00:01:30,423
So it begins by telling us that the Buddha is up in the Anguttara country and at the main city of Aparna.

11
00:01:30,763 --> 00:01:32,906
And there's also a venerable Udayin.

12
00:01:33,587 --> 00:01:35,810
And they both had a day's abiding.

13
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:40,034
Having gone out for their alms in the morning, they've had a day's abiding.

14
00:01:40,487 --> 00:01:43,052
And Udayin goes to see the Buddha.

15
00:01:44,414 --> 00:01:49,402
And before he goes that, he has these reflections.

16
00:01:49,421 --> 00:01:52,808
How many painful states has the Blessed One rid us of?

17
00:01:53,349 --> 00:01:57,093
How many pleasant states has the Blessed One brought us?

18
00:01:58,256 --> 00:01:58,576
Sorry.

19
00:01:59,718 --> 00:02:02,924
How many painful states has the Blessed One rid us of?

20
00:02:02,944 --> 00:02:06,849
How many pleasant states has the Blessed One brought us?

21
00:02:07,302 --> 00:02:16,116
How many unwholesome states has the Blessed One rid us of and how many wholesome states the Blessed One brought us?

22
00:02:17,418 --> 00:02:32,921
So he mentions this to the Buddha and he says, formally, now just to put you in the picture here, there would have been a time in the early order when a monk or nun would have gone out for food at any time

23
00:02:33,222 --> 00:02:40,811
as many times as they wanted to and would have eaten at any time of the day that they felt they wanted to eat.

24
00:02:41,372 --> 00:02:43,634
There was no rule about it.

25
00:02:43,655 --> 00:02:53,545
But then at some point, the lay people complained to the Buddha and said, look, your monks and nuns, they're coming around at all times, you know, and they're coming out more than once a day.

26
00:02:53,605 --> 00:03:00,715
So he made a rule that you had to go out once in the morning and eat by the midday.

27
00:03:03,157 --> 00:03:17,276
So now at this point, it would seem anyway that even though they were going out once in the morning and eating by midday, they would be able to keep some food for the evening.

28
00:03:18,198 --> 00:03:27,090
So here, venerable sir, formerly we used to eat in the evening, in the morning and during the day outside the proper time.

29
00:03:27,531 --> 00:03:31,216
Proper time here means the time that the Buddha then set afterwards.

30
00:03:31,235 --> 00:03:31,917
So they would eat

31
00:03:32,234 --> 00:03:35,579
morning, noon, and in the evening.

32
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:39,965
Now, it's got, it's got, yeah.

33
00:03:39,985 --> 00:03:42,769
Now, there was an occasion, the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus.

34
00:03:42,788 --> 00:03:43,229
Bhikkhus.

35
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:47,455
Please now, so bhikkhus here actually means the monastics.

36
00:03:47,475 --> 00:03:50,901
Please abandon that daytime meal outside the proper time.

37
00:03:51,641 --> 00:03:53,604
Now, I was upset, Udaym says.

38
00:03:53,865 --> 00:04:02,157
I was upset and sad thinking, faithful householders give us good food of various kinds during the day outside the allotted time.

39
00:04:02,322 --> 00:04:04,644
Yet the blessed one tells us to abandon it.

40
00:04:05,405 --> 00:04:07,407
The sublime one tells us to relinquish it.

41
00:04:08,907 --> 00:04:15,734
Out of our love and respect for the blessed one and out of shame and fear of wrongdoing, we abandon the daytime meal.

42
00:04:18,336 --> 00:04:22,560
Now, then we eat only in the evening and in the morning.

43
00:04:22,899 --> 00:04:28,165
So that was the first rule that you had to go out in the morning and eat it by midday.

44
00:04:29,165 --> 00:04:31,687
Now, remember, in the topics, it gets hot afternoons.

45
00:04:32,089 --> 00:05:00,072
after midday so it is good to eat before normally eating at 11 o'clock is great because when it gets too hot you go back to your cootie and have a rest but then the blessed one said please abandon the night meal which is outside the proper time and again the blessed one tells us to abandon more the more sumptuous of the two meals the sublime one tells us to relinquish it to put aside

46
00:05:00,456 --> 00:05:02,521
and we will all eat together.

47
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:03,783
Sorry.

48
00:05:04,603 --> 00:05:12,918
Once the venerable sir, a certain man would obtain soup during the day and he would say, put that aside and we will eat it together in the evening.

49
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:16,625
Nearly all cooking is done in the evening.

50
00:05:16,824 --> 00:05:18,668
little, little by day.

51
00:05:19,288 --> 00:05:28,040
So obviously at the end of a day's work, et cetera, and the coolness of the evening, remember it goes dark six is seven ish in, in the tropics.

52
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:32,346
Um, and that the days are pretty even throughout the year.

53
00:05:32,807 --> 00:05:38,675
So round about seven o'clock when the light's gone, then you'd start the cooking and you'd have your meal.

54
00:05:39,196 --> 00:05:44,502
And of course it would be the good meal of the day, but out of love and respect,

55
00:05:44,990 --> 00:05:51,718
For the Blessed One, out of shame for fear of wrongdoing, we abandoned the night meal, which was outside the proper time to be evening meal.

56
00:05:53,622 --> 00:06:08,161
As it happens, the bhikkhus, the monastics wandering for alms in the thick darkness of the night, have walked into a cesspit, fallen into a sewer, walked into a thorn bush and fallen over a sleeping cow.

57
00:06:08,918 --> 00:06:13,644
They have met hoodlums who have already committed a crime and those planning one.

58
00:06:14,105 --> 00:06:16,168
And they've been sexually enticed by women.

59
00:06:16,329 --> 00:06:17,269
So this is the main.

60
00:06:18,973 --> 00:06:19,173
Yes.

61
00:06:19,853 --> 00:06:23,559
Once, venerable sir, I went wandering for alms in the thick darkness of the night.

62
00:06:24,139 --> 00:06:31,750
And a woman washing a pot saw me by the flash of lightning and screamed out in terror, mercy me, a devil has come for me.

63
00:06:32,252 --> 00:06:34,194
And I told her, sister, I'm no devil.

64
00:06:34,675 --> 00:06:36,478
I'm a bhikkhu waiting for alms.

65
00:06:37,031 --> 00:06:40,276
Then it's a viku whose ma's dead or whose pa's died.

66
00:06:40,637 --> 00:06:41,858
Don't quite know the meaning of that.

67
00:06:42,319 --> 00:06:51,670
Better, viku, that you get your belly cut open with a sharp butcher's knife than this prowling for arms for your belly's sake in the thick darkness of the night.

68
00:06:51,711 --> 00:06:55,857
So this woman's very upset by being frightened by this monk.

69
00:06:56,918 --> 00:07:05,149
And so how, and because of all these little accidents that have been happening to the monastics,

70
00:07:05,415 --> 00:07:12,526
Presumably, that's why the Buddha banned it, asked them not to eat it in the evening, or at least that was one of the reasons.

71
00:07:14,689 --> 00:07:26,048
Now, there are some, of course, who were discourteous and did not follow the Buddha's rule.

72
00:07:26,067 --> 00:07:28,891
So he gives a couple of similes.

73
00:07:29,394 --> 00:07:41,387
So now supposing someone said a quail were tethered by a rotting creeper and would thereby expect injury, captivity or death.

74
00:07:42,588 --> 00:07:50,295
So would that quail be able to escape that rotting creeper?

75
00:07:50,935 --> 00:07:58,103
No, venerable sir, for the quail, the rotting creeper by which it is tethered and thereby expects injury, captivity or death

76
00:07:58,319 --> 00:08:02,723
is a strong, stout, tough, unrotting tether and a thick yoke.

77
00:08:03,845 --> 00:08:14,536
So for them, for such people, it becomes something that stops their progress.

78
00:08:16,358 --> 00:08:19,742
So that's one.

79
00:08:19,882 --> 00:08:24,968
So in other words, those monks and nuns who don't obey,

80
00:08:25,166 --> 00:08:29,992
They're like this quail caught by this rotting creeper.

81
00:08:31,593 --> 00:08:47,432
But then suppose a tusker elephant with tusks as long as chariot poles, full grown in stature, high bred and accustomed to battle, were tethered by stout leather thongs, but by simply twisting his body a little, he could break and burst the thongs and go where he likes.

82
00:08:47,972 --> 00:08:54,279
So in the same way, those people who honor the Buddha, they will release themselves from the tethers.

83
00:08:54,513 --> 00:09:01,580
The tether of all that comes with eating and not obeying the Buddha himself.

84
00:09:04,842 --> 00:09:13,130
And so having abandoned it, we live at ease, unruffled, subsisting on others' gifts, with mind as a wild deer's.

85
00:09:14,091 --> 00:09:18,355
For them, that thing becomes a feeble, weak, rotting, coreless tether.

86
00:09:18,855 --> 00:09:23,220
So in other words, they are relinquishing the desire

87
00:09:23,351 --> 00:09:24,513
to eat in the evening.

88
00:09:26,816 --> 00:09:28,378
And then he gives two other examples.

89
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:48,341
One is of a penniless destitute man who had one dilapidated hovel open to the crows, not the best kind, and one dilapidated wicker bedstead, not the best kind, and some grain and pumpkin seeds in a pot, not the best kind, and one hag of a wife, I didn't write this, not of the best kind.

90
00:09:49,241 --> 00:09:53,206
He might see a bhikkhu in the monastery park sitting in the shade of a tree,

91
00:09:53,490 --> 00:10:21,625
his hands and feet well washed after he had eaten a delicious meal devoting himself to the higher mind and he might think to himself oh how pleasant the reclusive state is how healthy the reclusive state is if only i could shave off my hair and beard put on the yellow robe and go forth from home to homelessness but he's unable to abandon what he has even though it's the dilapidated hobble and so forth and so he is weak a feeble weak

92
00:10:21,908 --> 00:10:24,556
rotting coreless, coreless tether.

93
00:10:26,422 --> 00:10:27,365
So he can't escape.

94
00:10:27,445 --> 00:10:33,825
On the other hand, we have a very rich merchant, great wealth and property with vast numbers of gold ingots.

95
00:10:34,379 --> 00:10:42,552
A vast number of granaries, a vast number of fields, a vast number of land, a vast number of wives, a vast number of men and women slaves.

96
00:10:43,293 --> 00:10:52,428
He might see the vicar in the monastery park sitting in the shade of his tree, his hands and feet well washed after he had eaten a delicious meal, devoting himself to the higher mind.

97
00:10:53,070 --> 00:10:58,158
And he might think how pleasant the reclusive state is, how healthy the reclusive state is.

98
00:10:58,730 --> 00:11:05,337
If only I could shave off my hair and beard, put on the yellow robe and go forth from home life into homelessness.

99
00:11:05,839 --> 00:11:11,505
And he is able to abandon his vast number of gold ingots, et cetera, et cetera.

100
00:11:12,346 --> 00:11:17,413
So it doesn't matter seemingly what you have, it's the ability to let go.

101
00:11:17,453 --> 00:11:25,563
So finally, we have these four types of people, these four types of persons.

102
00:11:26,336 --> 00:11:33,845
So someone who practices the way to abandoning, to the abandoning of attachment, to the relinquishing of attachment.

103
00:11:33,865 --> 00:11:39,850
And when he practices this way, memories and intentions associated with attachment beset him.

104
00:11:40,672 --> 00:11:41,653
He tolerates them.

105
00:11:41,692 --> 00:11:43,195
He does not abandon them.

106
00:11:43,495 --> 00:11:44,596
He does not remove them.

107
00:11:44,655 --> 00:11:47,899
He does not do away with them and annihilate them.

108
00:11:47,919 --> 00:11:52,884
Such a person I call fettered, not unfettered.

109
00:11:53,624 --> 00:11:59,711
Now, this actually refers to everybody, it seems, up to the state of non-returner.

110
00:12:00,030 --> 00:12:22,837
Remember those four levels of attainment, the stream entry, the person who's broken through the delusion of self, the once-returner, the person who is still attached to the sensual world, the anagami, the non-returner, the person who's let go of the sensual world, and finally, the arahat who's let go of everything.

111
00:12:23,779 --> 00:12:31,551
Then he says there's the person who practices the way abandoning of attachment to the relinquishment of attachment.

112
00:12:31,571 --> 00:12:36,557
When he's practicing this way, memories and intentions associated with attachment beset him.

113
00:12:37,298 --> 00:12:38,681
He does not tolerate them.

114
00:12:39,142 --> 00:12:39,982
He abandons them.

115
00:12:40,043 --> 00:12:42,986
He removes them, does away with them and annihilates them.

116
00:12:43,528 --> 00:12:46,011
And yet this person I call fettered.

117
00:12:46,312 --> 00:12:53,162
So here he's talking about that third stage of attainment, the anagami, the non-returner.

118
00:12:53,682 --> 00:13:01,910
And the next paragraph is exactly the same, except that the person suffers some sluggishness.

119
00:13:02,711 --> 00:13:09,739
His mindfulness may be slow in arising, but he quickly abandons them, removes them, does away with them, and annihilates them.

120
00:13:10,379 --> 00:13:22,913
Now, just as an aside, in a book by Jack Cornfield called Living Buddhist Masters, unfortunately, I think virtually all of them are dead, so it has been renamed, but if you type that in,

121
00:13:23,347 --> 00:13:28,092
you'll get the modern version of the modern title.

122
00:13:28,113 --> 00:13:35,039
And in it, he very beautifully lists all these various teachers, all the various techniques that they use.

123
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:37,361
And there's one person there called Sun Lun.

124
00:13:38,202 --> 00:13:40,546
And he was an uneducated farmer.

125
00:13:41,306 --> 00:13:45,230
And somebody came along and taught him how to do some breathing meditation.

126
00:13:45,951 --> 00:13:52,798
And he progressed so quickly that within a few months, he asked his wife if he could leave and become

127
00:13:53,081 --> 00:13:53,783
a monastic.

128
00:13:54,202 --> 00:13:57,225
And in so doing, he became a fully liberated person.

129
00:13:57,927 --> 00:14:02,812
And he was asked, he did all this in a matter of three to six months.

130
00:14:02,851 --> 00:14:06,095
I can't remember exactly, but extremely quickly.

131
00:14:06,414 --> 00:14:10,960
And when he was asked, why is it that people, you know, don't become fully liberated?

132
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:13,221
He says, because they're lazy.

133
00:14:14,643 --> 00:14:22,431
And I think if we look at our practice and if we're truthful with ourselves, we notice that we are at times sluggish.

134
00:14:24,503 --> 00:14:30,631
And then finally, of course, some person having understood that attachment is the root of suffering.

135
00:14:31,913 --> 00:14:42,570
Some person having understood that attachment is the root of suffering, divests himself of attachment and is liberated with the destruction of attachment.

136
00:14:42,590 --> 00:14:44,993
Such a person I call Ampheter.

137
00:14:45,374 --> 00:14:47,456
And that, of course, is the Arahant.

138
00:14:50,643 --> 00:14:59,155
So now the rest of the discourse is really just saying that one has to let go of sensual pleasures.

139
00:14:59,657 --> 00:15:04,923
And he doesn't have much of a liking for sensual pleasure.

140
00:15:05,365 --> 00:15:10,692
Pleasure, the pleasure and joy that arise dependent on the five chords of sensual pleasure.

141
00:15:11,062 --> 00:15:14,025
that's the five senses, are called sensual pleasure.

142
00:15:14,706 --> 00:15:18,591
A filthy pleasure, a coarse pleasure, an ignoble pleasure.

143
00:15:19,413 --> 00:15:28,583
I say of this kind of pleasure that it should not be pursued, that it should not be developed, that it should not be cultivated, and that it should be feared.

144
00:15:29,245 --> 00:15:36,615
So remember, the pleasures of the senses come as a given of the human condition.

145
00:15:37,054 --> 00:15:38,537
The problem is attachment.

146
00:15:38,855 --> 00:15:44,542
And therefore we should be afraid of falling into the error of attachment.

147
00:15:44,562 --> 00:15:46,124
That's what the Buddha means here.

148
00:15:47,044 --> 00:15:51,429
So one way of doing that is to suppress them by doing the jhanas.

149
00:15:51,450 --> 00:15:59,580
And this is what the whole next section is about, just going through the usual description of the eight jhana factors.

150
00:16:00,893 --> 00:16:10,267
And then finally, of course, do you see, Uday, in any fetter, small or great, of whose abandoning I do not speak?

151
00:16:10,648 --> 00:16:15,754
So basically, even when you get to all these jhanas, even they have to be abandoned.

152
00:16:16,336 --> 00:16:23,626
Anything to do with the phenomenal world, the world of the mind, the world of the body, eventually has to be abandoned.

153
00:16:24,873 --> 00:16:31,975
So that is the simile of the discourse is called the simile of the quail, which is only one of the similes.

154
00:16:32,719 --> 00:16:36,250
And it really is a sort of

155
00:16:36,684 --> 00:17:02,710
reminder to us that the path to liberation is the path of relinquishment the path of of letting go of attachments um we don't have to uh we don't have to berate ourselves because we occasionally fall into uh an indulgence uh but just to keep reminding ourselves that you know we've got to give this stuff up we've got to stop seeking happiness in the sensual world that's the trick

156
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:05,545
to stop seeking happiness in the sensual world.

157
00:17:06,346 --> 00:17:21,458
So to make that distinction between the simple pleasure that food gives us, but using food in order to make ourselves feel comfortable, comfort, in order to make ourselves feel excited,

158
00:17:22,130 --> 00:17:35,434
That's where the attachment manifests using food other than for feeding the body, nourishing the body and just allowing those pleasant tastes to arise and pass away.

159
00:17:35,836 --> 00:17:42,888
So don't forget when it comes to food at the end of a meal, you know, say to yourself, this is one less breakfast this last time.

160
00:17:43,138 --> 00:17:44,780
in this lifetime.

161
00:17:44,820 --> 00:17:47,345
This is one less lunch in this lifetime.

162
00:17:47,645 --> 00:17:50,890
This is one less evening meal in this lifetime.

163
00:17:51,171 --> 00:17:54,855
This is one less cup of tea in this lifetime.

164
00:17:55,396 --> 00:18:01,224
And slowly, just the drip, the drip of letting go will help us to relinquish everything.

165
00:18:01,625 --> 00:18:10,699
And in no length of time, we will be fully liberated from all suffering, hopefully sooner rather than later.

166
00:18:12,046 --> 00:18:13,720
so we can do a little bit of practice.

